"diminished_sixth" = "
Notice that there is a diminished sixth between A# and F. While it's enharmonic to a perfect Fifth, its diatonic function is different. Without introducing this interval, you cannot close the cycle, as the Perfect Fifth away from A# is E#, as demonstrated earlier.

This Circle of Fifths structure is useful for modulation, as closely related keys (adjacent on the circle) share many common tones, facilitating smoother key changes.
Also notice, that each note become warmer as it goes up in the spiral. This showcases, that modulations from C Major to G Major sounds more bright, than modulation in opposite direction.

